#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534110} :activity/id #uuid "1663c2eb-b22e-54c7-bdbc-48992d3d8048" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c39ae6-0e61-53a0-82d8-be231d625f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348813} {:answer/id #uuid "60f5725c-98e1-5e29-a6a2-1825e7d8a2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349332} {:answer/id #uuid "782d0dfd-d78f-5d74-a8ca-fe7e942c1a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349552} {:answer/id #uuid "97ede4f1-5ddb-5b1a-af4c-7752cd75112f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349807} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf6e5d4-325b-5104-8bc5-819d216a3de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348813} :answer-field/id #uuid "891eb5aa-802e-568e-97fe-54a14a5c4456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "20bf5820-0c11-5f18-a8d1-2aa12ca67619" :question/math-academy-id "q-81229" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} &=2{b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2} &=3{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2{b}_{2}-(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}+8{b}_{2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+8(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "1c597c4f-2892-5ffb-bea6-492cde3e3df6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19711b50-8807-59b7-acdb-8970429b0ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533199} {:answer/id #uuid "81206fce-7990-5565-942e-8da7f37d692c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533447} {:answer/id #uuid "8439ad9e-77a8-5942-943a-193cf7502f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533449} {:answer/id #uuid "86ce9780-060e-5652-a984-02fffec1697a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533458} {:answer/id #uuid "afc268a2-7c3e-5206-99f6-7894f6937564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533563}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533563} :answer-field/id #uuid "a12eb56b-c05a-51d2-a8e2-2e399e71b0cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4ad2c176-6641-542e-87e9-2e557825f205" :question/math-academy-id "q-129344" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}-4}{16-2(7)+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4}{16-14-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-y}-4)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{2x-y-16}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{16-2x+y}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\cancel{16-2x+y}}{\\cancel{(16-2x+y)}(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{8}.$"} :step/id #uuid "23b9f32a-54ea-563c-ae53-cfe97cf521f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534130}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d7b6960-ec55-5900-899c-bc269a25c30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533399} {:answer/id #uuid "8787e948-643c-527a-bb41-b899213c3f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533461} {:answer/id #uuid "8a308ba1-a690-5028-bd14-4b4c094a9be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533469} {:answer/id #uuid "c26e2d45-59a8-5ca1-907a-99bb13567a24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533608} {:answer/id #uuid "f92eca85-a0a5-5dec-acb0-377d28dc68d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533399} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdeec04-664e-562e-b615-6cc0242e52ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b213b7e1-d060-5456-8c0f-13babec4bbc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-80502" :question/problem "![](images/15/155e2eac58e35942918ef52682ebfdad4df283409372ac4a54d20218c363704e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "26cb25f8-7b7d-5dd5-8e96-152aa9c118e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62b7fc67-e9bb-5a0f-8e80-9dcf56649314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions is not commutative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396608} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd140b2-7871-5579-9e98-6ecf9f3f51ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition of functions is not associative" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396609} {:answer/id #uuid "1e058a54-9658-5798-ba7c-a86d9840498e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "addition and scalar multiplication of functions do not satisfy the distributive laws" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396610} {:answer/id #uuid "d6932b0d-e307-5cc8-971a-38fac820cb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\cdot f\\ne f$ for some $f\\in I$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396611} {:answer/id #uuid "4d629cc1-8c34-5761-b952-696843bcc069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$I$ does not contain the zero element" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396612} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ef701a-1be0-572c-baca-f76e42023271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "17d56701-363e-5629-ba43-f0ff54124002" :question/math-academy-id "q-106933" :question/problem "Let $I$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)={2}^{ax},$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$I$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **$I$ does not contain the zero element.** *\n\nIndeed, $f(x)={2}^{ax}>0$ for all $x\\in R$ for any $a\\in R,$ hence the zero function $z(x)=0\\notin I.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in I.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions  is commutative. $\\,✓$"} :step/id #uuid "53a90c3e-8ccd-531b-a24e-0020ecaffe9f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534130 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0125b04a-8d53-5abc-a1e6-91fa5ce4faf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)(5.4)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533132} {:answer/id #uuid "8c465360-9f49-5056-8848-9ec2535f007d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)^{3}(4.5)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533477} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7a2406-f122-5f65-af7c-47228282648b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)^{3}(5.4)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533479} {:answer/id #uuid "caae173f-731d-5732-a1d7-0aebb54d73b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)^{5.4}(4)^{-\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533628} {:answer/id #uuid "dd50d8d5-8574-52bd-8173-b2c211b302fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\theta +1)(4.5)^{\\theta -2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533479} :answer-field/id #uuid "169521b4-9bb4-5407-bcaa-8d03c32571c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b3b7675-fd44-5cc4-931a-4915684e1c8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159920" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $1.5,\\,1.8,\\,2.0.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +1){x}^{-\\theta -2},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +1){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -2} \\\\  & =(\\theta +1)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1.5\\cdot 1.8\\cdot 2=5.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +1)^{3}(5.4)^{-\\theta -2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "65b64849-ab70-599d-a259-4a06ae3aa38e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8d0b1e-9282-5f47-9e21-99f04d307001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533249} {:answer/id #uuid "3789e1cb-7526-534c-8e9d-c57c1b845ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533270} {:answer/id #uuid "494af595-cebb-5661-be3d-fe3be8ea93c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533311} {:answer/id #uuid "ca95a4d2-2d19-5883-bde3-2f2809aa0baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533627} {:answer/id #uuid "e56d8531-f316-56f3-a5c0-ed11ea1ce780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533704} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecec1431-9990-543a-b776-c6ce2fb531c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7429a73e-417e-56af-b923-b010e58f428c" :question/math-academy-id "q-174370" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\},\\,B=\\{3n∣n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\{1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…\\}$\n\nII. $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{3n∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,6,9,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B=\\{1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…\\}\\cap \\{3,6,9,…\\}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "7511bf7c-b967-5a09-a842-e86f744cecdf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd0bea6-68a0-58ce-8634-70c307993224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533254} {:answer/id #uuid "807b5135-d195-5b06-9f63-c2e1977f3b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533444} {:answer/id #uuid "95df5cd1-cd39-5cc5-8934-32cfb4f72d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533502} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e972e5-b57b-5035-9e5e-a84235d6cc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533673} {:answer/id #uuid "e14497f7-1248-5455-adb6-b15bc0a88d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533673} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b74b3d-081b-5dbc-b46b-ea9f6c7c969a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9db48f80-e375-5a5e-b7bd-dd584b0ae10d" :question/math-academy-id "q-175365" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $P(x,y):$ $x+y<1$\n\nII. $Q(z):$ $\\frac{6}{z}=8$\n\nIII. $R(n):$ $-n\\in N$" :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $z$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "f865d499-2710-534b-8e5c-137aeb6c6656" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534123}}] :activity/title "Quiz 9" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533119}]}
